+//! Translation of Codex permission profiles into an OpenBSD
+//! [`unveil(2)`](https://man.openbsd.org/unveil.2) policy.
+//!
+//! The translation is intentionally platform independent and is compiled on
+//! every host so that it can be unit tested without an OpenBSD kernel. The
+//! code that actually applies the translated policy — and the only code that
+//! depends on the `rust-openbsd` bindings — lives in [`crate::openbsd`], which
+//! is compiled only for `target_os = "openbsd"`.
+//!
+//! # Why the translation can fail
+//!
+//! `unveil(2)` is a *whitelist*: once the first rule is installed, the entire
+//! filesystem is hidden except for the unveiled paths. A rule that matches a
+//! path is only shadowed by a more specific matching rule at a lower level, so
+//! a writable root can be carved back to read-only for selected descendants.
+//! The standard "read everything, write a set of roots" and "read everything,
+//! write nothing" profiles map to these path rules. This implementation only
+//! supports profiles with a full-disk read grant, optionally narrowed by exact
+//! path denials. Narrow read allowlists and glob denials are rejected rather
+//! than approximated. Descriptor-based operations have separate kernel semantics
+//! (see the port's maintenance notes).
+
